window.KillerBenchEngineDocs = {
  "overview": "# The KillerBench.Engine\n\nThe KillerBench.Engine is a Windows diagnostics and repair library for applications that need structured evidence instead of a collection of opaque scripts. It powers the KillerBench desktop and command-line tools, and it can be embedded in another .NET host.\n\n## What it provides\n\n- Bounded diagnostic workflows for Windows servicing, devices, storage, networking, printing, security, applications, profiles, and other system areas\n- Typed evidence with explicit complete, incomplete, unknown, and historical states\n- Repair recipes that require exact current evidence before they can run\n- Durable journals for repair verification and interrupted-run recovery\n- Progress callbacks for live displays such as servicing activity\n- Text, JSON, HTML, and structured .NET results for reports and integrations\n- Versioned knowledge packs for error codes, hardware identifiers, vendors, and support references\n\n## Designed for traceability\n\nA finding is tied to the evidence that produced it. Historical events stay historical. Missing data does not become a healthy verdict. A failed provider does not become an empty result. The Engine reports the boundary of what it inspected so a host can explain what is known and what remains unknown.\n\n## Diagnostics first\n\nMost Engine operations are read-only. Repair support is deliberately narrower than diagnostic coverage. A repair must have a supported recipe, fresh eligibility evidence, explicit host authorization, a durable preparation record, a bounded native action, and post-action verification.\n\n## Current status\n\nThe Engine targets .NET 10 and Windows. It is in active development and is currently consumed through a project reference inside the KillerBench repository. A public package has not been released.\n\n## Choose a guide\n\n- [Getting started](getting-started.html) shows a first read-only workflow.\n- [Diagnostic workflows](workflows.html) explains focused checks and the full system scan.\n- [Evidence and results](evidence.html) explains states, journals, progress, and reports.\n- [Repairs and safety](repairs.html) describes the evidence gates and recovery model.\n- [CLI guide](cli.html) covers command discovery, text output, and JSON output.\n- [API guide](api.html) maps the main public entry points.\n- [Architecture](architecture.html) explains the Engine boundary and internal layers.\n- [Knowledge packs](knowledge-packs.html) covers updateable reference data.\n",
  "getting-started": "# Getting started\n\nRun a read-only diagnostic workflow and inspect its typed result in a few minutes.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Windows 10 or Windows 11\n- .NET 10 SDK\n- A .NET 10 application\n- A writable folder for workflow journals\n\nThe Engine has no WPF dependency. The desktop interface is a separate host.\n\n## Reference the project\n\nAdd the Engine project to your solution, then reference it from your application:\n\n```xml\n<ProjectReference Include=\"path\\to\\KillerBench\\Engine\\KillerBench.Engine.csproj\" />\n```\n\n## Run a focused workflow\n\n```csharp\nusing KillerBench.Engine;\n\nstring journalFolder = Path.Combine(\n    Environment.GetFolderPath(Environment.SpecialFolder.LocalApplicationData),\n    \"ExampleHost\",\n    \"Workflows\");\n\nvar progress = new Progress<string>(Console.WriteLine);\n\nWorkflowResult result = await Workflows.RunAsync(\n    WorkflowKind.Network,\n    autoRepair: false,\n    journalFolder,\n    progress,\n    CancellationToken.None);\n\nConsole.WriteLine(result.Status);\n\nforeach (WorkflowCheck check in result.Checks)\n{\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{check.State}: {check.Name}\");\n    Console.WriteLine(check.Detail);\n}\n```\n\n`autoRepair: false` keeps the run diagnostic-only. The Engine writes a workflow journal even when no repair is attempted.\n\n## Run the broad diagnostic scan\n\n```csharp\nWorkflowResult result = await FullSystemScan.RunAsync(\n    journalFolder,\n    autoRepair: false,\n    progress,\n    CancellationToken.None);\n```\n\nThe full scan combines common diagnostic areas. It does not prove every possible Windows component healthy, and it does not enable every optional repair.\n\n## Handle cancellation\n\nPass a `CancellationToken` from your host. The Engine checks cancellation between bounded stages. If a repair may already have reached Windows, the durable journal remains available for reconciliation instead of silently retrying the action.\n\n## Next steps\n\n- Read [Diagnostic workflows](workflows.html) before selecting workflow kinds.\n- Read [Repairs and safety](repairs.html) before exposing any repair action.\n- Use the [CLI guide](cli.html) to explore diagnostics without writing a host application.\n",
  "workflows": "# Diagnostic workflows\n\nWorkflows collect related evidence and return one `WorkflowResult`. They are the simplest high-level Engine surface for desktop applications, automation hosts, and support tools.\n\n## Focused workflows\n\n`WorkflowKind` identifies a diagnostic area. Current groups include:\n\n| Group | Examples |\n| --- | --- |\n| Servicing | Windows Update, system files, component cleanup, installer, servicing activity |\n| Hardware | Devices, storage, audio, camera, Bluetooth, display, input |\n| Network | Network, wireless, wired authentication, firewall services, file sharing |\n| Windows services | BITS, Cryptographic Services, Windows Modules Installer, Task Scheduler, WinRM |\n| User environment | Profiles, applications, startup applications, Appx packages, privacy |\n| Recovery, backup and security | Backup services, Defender, BitLocker, System Restore, Windows Recovery, certificates |\n| Reliability | Vitals, reliability history, hardware errors, crash evidence, event logs |\n\nThe enum is intentionally broader than the repair catalog. A diagnostic workflow can exist without a safe automatic repair.\n\n## Check states\n\n| State | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Clear` | Complete evidence matched the workflow's healthy condition |\n| `Repairable` | Complete current evidence matched a supported repair recipe |\n| `Attention` | Evidence deserves review, but it does not authorize a repair by itself |\n| `Unknown` | Collection or interpretation was incomplete, contradictory, truncated, or unavailable |\n| `Information` | Context or history that is not a current fault verdict |\n\nHistorical warnings and errors usually produce `Attention`, not `Repairable`. An empty event history can be useful context, but it does not certify current health.\n\n## Combined readiness conclusions\n\nDevice Association combines bounded Device Setup Manager history, Plug and Play configuration history and the exact service conclusion. Historical warnings remain review context when the current service is available. The result does not claim that discovery, pairing, association, driver installation or device operation works.\n\nThe focused Function Discovery Provider Host and Resource Publication workflows combine current network profile assignments with the exact service conclusion. A Public profile or current service concern requests review, while incomplete evidence remains Unknown. The results do not prove provider health, discovery, publication, visibility, firewall effectiveness, share access or connectivity.\n\nWindows Font Cache combines bounded machine and current-user font registrations with the exact service conclusion. Missing registered files or a current service concern request review, while incomplete evidence remains Unknown. The result does not parse fonts, inspect cache contents, render text, authorize a cache rebuild or prove application behavior.\n\nApp Readiness, State Repository, AppX Deployment, Client License, Microsoft Store installation, Windows License Manager, PushToInstall, User Profile, Device Installation and Optimize Drives also return feature-specific conclusions. AppX history provides bounded context for readiness, repository and deployment services. Microsoft Store history provides bounded context for installation, licensing and remote-install services. Event-backed workflows keep retained warnings historical when the exact service is currently available. Optimize Drives treats the exact ScheduledDefrag task as current evidence. Incomplete feature or service evidence takes precedence, and none of these conclusions selects a package, license, request, profile, device, task, drive or service repair.\n\nMACE provides a review-only Microsoft application removal plan for explicit OneDrive, Outlook, Office/Microsoft 365, Project and Teams selections. Alongside exact registration evidence, it queries a fixed set of executable names for selected products and retains bounded counts only. Active or incomplete process evidence blocks future removal eligibility. The Engine does not expose process identities or authorize removal from this review.\n\n## Full system scan\n\n`FullSystemScan.RunAsync` uses the common diagnostic catalog. Hosts can pass selected diagnostic scopes when they want a smaller run. The result records which checks actually ran and preserves unknown coverage rather than filling gaps with assumptions.\n\n## Live servicing activity\n\n`ServicingActivityMonitor.ObserveAsync` is the Engine boundary for PEEK-style live Windows Update and Setup visibility. It emits bounded snapshots with process activity, recognized update transitions, setup mode, compatibility counts, readiness evidence, and optional histories.\n\nThe monitor reports observed state. It does not invent a percentage, declare a stall from elapsed time alone, or authorize a repair.\n\n## Progress and status\n\nPass `IProgress<string>` to surface the current stage in a desktop status area, console, or log. Progress messages are for live presentation. The final `WorkflowResult` remains the durable record.\n\n## Workflow journals\n\nEvery workflow receives a journal folder. The journal records the machine, workflow kind, start time, checks, repair outcomes, recipes, and final status. A saved journal is historical evidence. Reopening it never turns recorded claims into a fresh machine check.\n",
  "evidence": "# Evidence and results\n\nThe Engine separates provider output, interpreted checks, workflow results, and human-readable reports. Each layer keeps uncertainty visible.\n\n## Provider evidence\n\nNative commands and Windows providers run behind fixed time, record, and size bounds. `ToolResult` records exit status, timeout, truncation, standard output, and error output. A provider failure or truncated result cannot be treated as a clean empty response.\n\nCollectors minimize retained data. Many diagnostic results keep names, categories, counts, states, and timestamps while excluding credentials, command lines, full paths, account identities, network endpoints, and unrelated event payloads.\n\n## Workflow checks\n\n`WorkflowCheck` adds a name, interpreted state, explanation, optional repair kind, and optional provider evidence. Consumers should display the state and detail together. The state alone does not communicate the scope or limitations of the check.\n\n## Workflow results\n\n`WorkflowResult` contains:\n\n- The workflow kind and machine\n- Start time and final status\n- Every completed check\n- Any repair results\n- Recipe results\n- The saved journal path\n\nUse the typed result for UI decisions. Use reports for people and JSON for interoperable automation.\n\n## Reports\n\nKillerBench can generate diagnostic-only or diagnostic-and-repair reports. The report separates:\n\n1. What was inspected\n2. What problem was found\n3. What evidence supports the finding\n4. What action was proposed or performed\n5. How the result was verified\n6. What remains unknown or needs a restart\n\nHTML reports use the Killer Tools theme system and remain readable without the desktop app. Text reports favor portability. JSON retains structured fields for another program.\n\n## Historical evidence\n\nEvent records and imported logs describe what was recorded at a past time. They can identify useful patterns, error codes, and the next diagnostic scope. They do not prove that the fault is still active.\n\n## Unknown is a result\n\n`Unknown` is not an exception to hide. It tells the host that access was denied, a source was missing, a provider failed, output was malformed, the bound was exceeded, or evidence disagreed. A useful host shows this limitation and offers the relevant next step.\n",
  "repairs": "# Repairs and safety\n\nKillerBench repairs are typed operations with eligibility, execution, verification, and recovery rules. They are not arbitrary command strings selected from a log line.\n\n## Repair sequence\n\n1. Collect complete current evidence.\n2. Match one supported recipe.\n3. Review required options and consequences.\n4. Confirm administrative and policy prerequisites.\n5. Recollect the decisive evidence immediately before mutation.\n6. Write a durable preparation record.\n7. Run one bounded native action.\n8. Collect fresh verification evidence.\n9. Record a verified, blocked, stopped, or uncertain outcome.\n\nIf the decisive evidence changes before execution, the Engine stops without performing the action.\n\n## Explicit choices\n\nOption-dependent repairs require exact choices from the host. Examples include a selected volume, printer queue, recovery-key destination, power values, package version, or repair source. The generic automatic runner cannot invent these values.\n\n## Managed systems\n\nGroup Policy, device management, and other authority evidence can make a local repair ineffective or inappropriate. Supported coordinators inspect applicable authority state and fail closed when ownership is unknown or managed.\n\n## Durable pending records\n\nA process can stop after Windows receives a request but before verification completes. In that case the Engine retains a pending record. A later reconciliation reads fresh state and determines what happened. Reconciliation never repeats the original mutation automatically.\n\n## Automatic repair\n\n`autoRepair` means the host allows eligible recipes to proceed. It does not bypass evidence requirements, choose missing options, ignore policy, retry an uncertain action, restart Windows, or turn an `Attention` finding into a repair.\n\n## Host responsibility\n\nBefore enabling a repair, a host should show:\n\n- The exact target\n- The proposed change\n- Required elevation or restart\n- Data and settings that remain untouched\n- The evidence that made the action eligible\n- The journal and recovery state after execution\n\nUse disposable virtual machines to validate native mutations across Windows versions and failure states before exposing them to production users.\n",
  "cli": "# CLI guide\n\n`killerbench-cli` exposes the Engine for technicians, scripts, test harnesses, and headless collection.\n\n## Build\n\n```powershell\ndotnet build Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj\n```\n\n## Discover commands\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- help\n```\n\nThe help output is the authoritative command list for the current checkout. Commands include focused diagnostics, complete workflows, servicing activity, knowledge lookups, planning tools, report generation, and selected guarded repairs.\n\n## Run a workflow\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow Network\n```\n\nAdd `--json` when a command supports structured output:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow Network --json\n```\n\n## Run focused diagnostics\n\nFocused commands are useful when a host needs one evidence boundary instead of a full workflow. Examples include:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- firewall-events\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- servicing-service-events\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- memory-diagnostic-results\n```\n\n## Live servicing monitor\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- servicing-monitor\n```\n\nThe monitor prints completed snapshots as they arrive. It reports recognized activity and evidence, not an invented completion percentage.\n\n## Export the VM validation plan\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-plan\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-plan --workflow update\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-plan --recipe windows.system-files\n```\n\nThe command returns deterministic JSON scenario requirements for the installed recipe catalog. It runs no diagnostics or repairs. The output is a disposable-VM test plan, not evidence that a native scenario passed.\n\nValidate records returned from disposable VMs and summarize current-version coverage:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-record RECORD.json\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-coverage RECORD.json MORE-RECORDS.json\n```\n\nRecords must match the installed recipe version and scenario ID and contain every required artifact label and SHA-256 digest. Coverage keeps Passed, Failed, Blocked, Inconclusive and missing scenarios separate. These commands read the record files only.\n\n## Execution boundary\n\nMost commands are read-only. Commands that can change Windows require their exact execution switch and still enforce the same Engine eligibility, journaling, final-evidence, and verification rules. Unsupported execution is rejected.\n\n## Exit behavior\n\nTreat JSON fields and documented exit behavior as the automation contract. Do not parse decorative console text when structured output is available. Preserve the journal path for later review or reconciliation.\n\n",
  "api": "# API guide\n\nThe Engine uses one public namespace, `KillerBench.Engine`, while source folders organize the implementation by capability.\n\n## High-level entry points\n\n| Task | Start with |\n| --- | --- |\n| Run one diagnostic area | `Workflows.RunAsync` and `WorkflowKind` |\n| Run common diagnostics | `FullSystemScan.RunAsync` |\n| Show available common areas | `CommonDiagnosticCatalog.All` |\n| Show repair recipes | `RecipeCatalog.ForWorkflow` |\n| Observe servicing activity | `ServicingActivityMonitor.ObserveAsync` |\n| Interpret a completed workflow | `WorkflowResult` and `WorkflowRecoveryPlan.From` |\n| Review a saved journal | `WorkflowJournalReview` |\n| Generate reports | Engine report and export types |\n\n## Core result types\n\n| Type | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `WorkflowCheck` | One interpreted diagnostic result with state, detail, evidence, and optional repair |\n| `WorkflowResult` | Complete workflow output plus journal location |\n| `ToolResult` | Bounded native-provider result including timeout and truncation state |\n| `RepairRecipe` | Eligibility and execution metadata for one supported repair |\n| `RecipeResult` | Outcome of evaluating or executing a recipe |\n| `RepairResult` | Native repair steps, verification, and durable outcome |\n\n## Capability folders\n\n| Folder | Responsibility |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Applications` | Installed applications, packages, updates, and deployment evidence |\n| `Audio`, `Devices`, `Storage` | Hardware and device diagnostics |\n| `BitLocker`, `Security`, `Backup` | Protection, recovery, and security state |\n| `Network`, `Printing`, `Time` | Connectivity and peripheral workflows |\n| `Installation`, `Updates`, `Services` | Servicing, setup, event, and service diagnostics |\n| `Profiles`, `FileSystem`, `Cleanup`, `Debloat` | User environment and maintenance planning |\n| `Evidence`, `Knowledge` | Imported evidence, catalogs, and deterministic interpretation |\n| `Workflows`, `Automation` | High-level orchestration and script-derived planning surfaces |\n| `Infrastructure` | Bounded tools, persistence, recovery, and shared provider helpers |\n\n## Cancellation and progress\n\nLong-running public operations accept `CancellationToken`. Workflow and monitor entry points also accept `IProgress<string>`. A host should cancel through the token and keep the final or pending journal. It should not terminate the process merely to stop an operation that has a supported cancellation path.\n\n## Lower-level collectors\n\nCapability-specific collectors are public when another host can use them safely without running a complete workflow. Their result records describe the exact data retained and whether collection was complete. Consult the source and the detailed capability catalog for the current surface.\n\n## Versioning\n\nThe Engine currently shares KillerBench version `0.1.0`. It has not reached a stable public package contract. Pin the exact repository revision used by an embedding application.\n",
  "architecture": "# Architecture\n\nThe KillerBench.Engine is a UI-free project inside the KillerBench repository. The desktop, CLI, and tests reference it as separate hosts.\n\n## Boundary\n\nThe Engine owns:\n\n- Bounded Windows evidence collection\n- Deterministic interpretation and correlation\n- Workflow and recipe catalogs\n- Repair eligibility and execution coordination\n- Durable journals and no-repeat reconciliation\n- Report models and knowledge-pack readers\n\nThe host owns:\n\n- Navigation, dialogs, controls, and presentation\n- User choices and consent\n- UAC relaunch behavior\n- Progress and cancellation controls\n- File pickers and report destinations\n- Product updates and deployment\n\n## Project relationships\n\n```text\nKillerBench desktop\n        |\n        v\nKillerBench.Engine <---- killerbench-cli\n        ^\n        |\nKillerBench.Checks\n```\n\nThe Engine does not reference WPF or desktop controls. Tests can substitute provider and repair hosts so parsers, eligibility, safety gates, interruption behavior, and recovery can be verified without changing the development computer.\n\n## Internal layers\n\n| Layer | Responsibility |\n| --- | --- |\n| Capability collectors | Read bounded Windows state for one exact scope |\n| Evidence parsers | Validate shape, identity, completeness, and limits |\n| Diagnostic interpretation | Convert evidence into explicit workflow states |\n| Workflow orchestration | Combine checks, progress, recipes, and journals |\n| Repair coordinators | Recheck eligibility, prepare, mutate, verify, and reconcile |\n| Reports | Present problems, actions, results, and limitations |\n| Knowledge packs | Resolve versioned external reference data without changing logic |\n\n## Windows and shell modes\n\nThe current native provider layer targets Windows and uses .NET, Windows APIs, PowerShell, and Windows command-line tools where appropriate. The Engine is not cross-platform today.\n\nA future Bash provider would be a separate host implementation behind explicit interfaces. It would need its own capability catalog, evidence contracts, safety rules, and tests. Translating PowerShell command strings into Bash would not create equivalent diagnostics.\n\n## Failure model\n\nCollectors fail closed when identity is ambiguous, output is malformed, a limit is exceeded, or required authority cannot be inspected. Repair coordinators separate preview, preparation, dispatch, verification, and reconciliation so an interrupted process does not automatically repeat a machine change.\n",
  "knowledge-packs": "# Knowledge packs\n\nKnowledge packs keep large and frequently updated reference data outside the Engine's diagnostic logic.\n\n## Suitable data\n\n- Windows and Setup error codes\n- Vendor and device identifiers\n- PCI, USB, ACPI, and UEFI references\n- Network OUI vendor data\n- Driver and support locations\n- Application and package metadata\n- Curated support links and diagnostic scopes\n\n## Pack contract\n\nA pack should identify its format, schema version, source, source revision, generation time, and content hash. The Engine validates those fields before using the data. Unknown or invalid packs remain unavailable rather than silently falling back to guesses.\n\n## Generators\n\nGenerators belong in the repository when they are required to reproduce a distributed pack. They should:\n\n1. Download only from named authoritative sources.\n2. Record source revisions and licenses.\n3. Validate input before transformation.\n4. Normalize data deterministically.\n5. Write a manifest and content hash.\n6. Produce the same output from the same source input.\n\nLarge generated databases and temporary downloads do not belong in the Engine source folder. The repository can contain small fixtures for parser and schema tests.\n\n## Updates\n\nThe desktop About surface can offer an Update databases action. An update should download to a temporary location, validate the manifest and hash, then replace the active pack atomically. A failed update must leave the prior valid pack available.\n\n## Separation from diagnostics\n\nKnowledge data can explain an identifier or suggest a next diagnostic scope. It cannot convert a historical code into a current fault, choose a driver automatically, or authorize a repair. Current machine evidence remains the deciding input.\n"
};
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli = "# CLI guide\n\n`killerbench-cli` exposes the Engine for technicians, scripts, test harnesses, and headless collection.\n\n## Build\n\n```powershell\ndotnet build Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj\n```\n\n## Discover commands\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- help\n```\n\nThe help output is the authoritative command list for the current checkout. Commands include focused diagnostics, complete workflows, servicing activity, knowledge lookups, planning tools, report generation, and selected guarded repairs.\n\n## Run a workflow\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow Network\n```\n\nAdd `--json` when a command supports structured output:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow Network --json\n```\n\n## Run focused diagnostics\n\nFocused commands are useful when a host needs one evidence boundary instead of a full workflow. Examples include:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- firewall-events\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- servicing-service-events\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- memory-diagnostic-results\n```\n\n## Live servicing monitor\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- servicing-monitor\n```\n\nThe monitor prints completed snapshots as they arrive. It reports recognized activity and evidence, not an invented completion percentage.\n\n## Export the VM validation plan\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-plan\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-plan --workflow update\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-plan --recipe windows.system-files\n```\n\nThe command returns deterministic JSON scenario requirements for the installed recipe catalog. It runs no diagnostics or repairs. The output is a disposable-VM test plan, not evidence that a native scenario passed.\n\nValidate records returned from disposable VMs and summarize current-version coverage:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-record RECORD.json\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-coverage RECORD.json MORE-RECORDS.json\n```\n\nRecords must match the installed recipe version and scenario ID and contain every required artifact label and SHA-256 digest. Coverage keeps Passed, Failed, Blocked, Inconclusive and missing scenarios separate. These commands read the record files only.\n\nGenerate a readable local coverage report from the same validated records:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-report coverage.html RECORD.json MORE-RECORDS.json\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- validation-report coverage.txt RECORD.json\n```\n\nHTML reports include every installed scenario, status and text filters, and the 13 Killer Tools themes. Missing evidence remains distinct from every recorded outcome. Use `--theme`, `--accent` and `--overwrite` when needed. Report generation does not inspect or change Windows.\n\n## Execution boundary\n\nMost commands are read-only. Commands that can change Windows require their exact execution switch and still enforce the same Engine eligibility, journaling, final-evidence, and verification rules. Unsupported execution is rejected.\n\n## Exit behavior\n\nTreat JSON fields and documented exit behavior as the automation contract. Do not parse decorative console text when structured output is available. Preserve the journal path for later review or reconciliation.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\n## Discover workflow controls\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options servicing-monitor\n```\n\nThe command returns host-neutral JSON for the option-rich workflows. It includes toggles, sliders, choices, text fields, defaults, bounds, dependencies and the execution boundary. It does not inspect or change Windows.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\n## Workflow controls\n\n`WorkflowOptionCatalog` lets a desktop, CLI or embedded host discover the same supported controls without copying UI definitions. Each option has a stable key, label, control type, default value and explanation. Sliders include their minimum, maximum and step. Choices expose exact values. Conditional controls identify the option and value that enables them.\n\n`ChangesMachine` is true only for an implemented explicit execution control. These controls are advanced and off by default. The flag describes the control surface; it does not bypass fresh evidence, eligibility, journaling, verification or recovery requirements.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\nValidate a proposed plan without running it:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options servicing-monitor --set refreshSeconds=5 --set samples=4\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options network --set tcpHost=127.0.0.1 --set tcpPort=443 --set timeoutSeconds=15\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options defender --set scanType=Quick --set startScan=true\n```\n\nRepeated `--set KEY=VALUE` arguments are normalized and checked against the shared Engine definitions. The result identifies validation errors and whether a machine-changing control was requested. No Windows inspection or execution occurs.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\nPass submitted key/value pairs to `WorkflowOptionValidation.Validate`. It fills omitted defaults, normalizes booleans and integers, and rejects unknown or duplicate keys, invalid choices, out-of-range or off-step sliders, oversized text, missing required values and inactive conditional controls. A valid machine-changing request still authorizes nothing. The operation-specific coordinator must collect fresh evidence and enforce its own execution boundary.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\n## Run validated workflow controls\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow network --set lookupHost=example.com --set timeoutSeconds=15\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow profiles --set minimumInactiveDays=180 --set checkDependencies=true --json\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow servicing-monitor --set refreshSeconds=5 --set samples=4\n```\n\nThe workflow command consumes the normalized shared plan and runs the matching read-only diagnostic. It refuses auto-repair, recipe selection and every machine-changing option. The first result records the nondefault settings in effect while redacting or counting sensitive paths, switches, identifiers, package sources and deployment device names. Prepared System Restore settings drive the creation-readiness review. Selected filesystem repair settings collect exact-volume state and bounded read-only diagnosis, then explain backup, restart or dismount requirements without scheduling repair. Selected storage optimization settings drive exact-volume analysis. Selected printing targets correlate one exact queue and optional job with current bounded evidence. A selected MSI product is reviewed against exact registration, Installer activity, restart and administrator evidence. A selected Windows Time source is reviewed against current role, policy, mode and peer evidence. Dedicated guarded commands remain required for repairs.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\n`WorkflowOptionProjection.CreateHost` converts a valid read-only plan into the same `WindowsWorkflowHost` used by the app and CLI. It prepends a redacted `Selected workflow options` result, applies prepared System Restore settings to creation-readiness review, applies selected filesystem repair settings to exact-volume state, diagnosis and consequence review, applies selected storage optimization settings to exact-volume analysis, correlates selected printing targets with current queue and job evidence, reviews a selected MSI product against current registration, Installer activity, restart and administrator evidence, and reviews a selected Windows Time source against current role, policy, mode and peer evidence. `CreateServicingOptions` projects PEEK controls into `ServicingMonitorOptions`. Both reject invalid plans and every plan that requests a machine change.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Focused Backup Services conclusion\n\nThe Backup Services workflow returns one focused conclusion across Windows Backup and File History observations, VSS and volsnap history, current VSS writers, provider registrations, snapshots, shadow storage, exact service histories, administrator state and restart state. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical records alone remain informational, and the conclusion does not prove that a backup is configured, current, restorable or successful.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Vitals stability conclusion\n\nThe Vitals workflow returns one system-stability conclusion across live memory, disk and uptime snapshots, Windows Memory Diagnostic results, current device problems, reliability history, hardware-error records, crash evidence and unexpected shutdowns. It keeps incomplete coverage visible and does not claim that hardware, software, drivers, firmware or future operation are stable.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Applications stability-history conclusion\n\nThe Applications workflow combines the bounded Application error log with Reliability Monitor history and returns one stability-history conclusion. It keeps collection gaps explicit and uses retained crash or hang records only to choose a narrower diagnostic area. Historical records do not become a current fault or automatic repair.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Startup environment conclusion\n\nThe Startup Applications workflow returns one environment conclusion across registration names, Windows approval markers and boot or logon scheduled-task identities. It keeps incomplete sources visible while excluding commands, arguments, accounts and credentials. The result does not measure startup cost or decide that an item is safe to disable.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Defender scan history\n\nThe Microsoft Defender workflow includes bounded quick and full scan history. It retains timestamps, provider age and aggregate scan-in-progress state while excluding threats, paths, resources, accounts, exclusions and file contents. Missing history is informational, overdue provider flags request review and incomplete evidence remains unknown. The `defender-scan-history` command exposes the same read-only result and never starts a scan.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Explicit Defender scans\n\n`DefenderScanExecution` keeps Quick and Full scans separate from automatic repair recipes. It requires an explicit type and execution request, administrator access, clear restart state, active Defender protection and complete idle scan evidence. It writes a durable guard before dispatch, repeats eligibility, and verifies only a newer matching completion timestamp. Reconciliation observes current history and never starts another scan. The CLI commands are `defender-scan Quick|Full [--execute]` and `defender-scan-recover`.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Scheduled task operational conclusion\n\nThe Scheduled Tasks workflow returns one operational conclusion across bounded task runtime results, missed-run counts and exact Task Scheduler service failure history. Incomplete inventory or history prevents a combined conclusion. Task-specific results and service failures request narrower review without starting, enabling, disabling or changing a task or service.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## App package operational conclusion\n\nThe App Packages workflow returns one operational conclusion across current registered-package state, optional provisioning inventory, restart state, AppX deployment history and Microsoft Store installation history. Incomplete sources remain explicit. Historical failures direct narrower transaction review without becoming a current package fault or authorizing a repair.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Device operational conclusion\n\nThe Device Problems workflow returns one operational conclusion across current nonzero Plug and Play configuration codes, exact retained SetupAPI driver-install correlations, recent device setup history and recent Plug and Play history. Incomplete sources prevent a combined conclusion. Historical failures direct narrower identity-based review without becoming a current device fault or authorizing a driver change.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Installed application operational conclusion\n\nThe Installed Applications workflow preserves its machine or current-account scope while combining the selected uninstall registrations with bounded Application error history, Reliability Monitor history and restart state. Registration and broad stability history are not treated as an exact identity match. The conclusion directs narrower diagnosis without launching, repairing or removing an application.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Certificate operational conclusion\n\nThe Certificates workflow returns one operational conclusion across bounded personal-certificate validity and cache-only local chain evidence from the machine and current-user personal stores. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Validity or local-chain concerns request exact certificate review without changing trust, enrollment, bindings or private keys.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Windows activation operational conclusion\n\nThe Windows Activation workflow returns one operational conclusion across current Windows licensing and sanitized Software Protection history. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Current licensing concerns request review, while historical warnings remain informational when the current provider reports Licensed. No activation, product-key, token, license, account, service or policy action is selected.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Storage Spaces operational conclusion\n\nThe Storage Spaces workflow returns one operational conclusion across bounded pool and virtual-disk health evidence. Incomplete evidence takes precedence, reported health or operational-state concerns request exact identity review, and a complete empty inventory remains informational. No pool, disk, volume, allocation, attachment, repair, optimization, resiliency or setting operation is selected.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Profile review conclusion\n\nThe Profiles workflow returns separate inventory, exact ProfileList registration and optional service or scheduled-task account dependency results plus one bounded review conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Registration or dependency concerns require narrower identity-based review. Age and snapshot checks never authorize removal or prove that data is backed up, restorable or unused.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Startup performance conclusion\n\nThe Startup Performance workflow returns one conclusion across sanitized startup and shutdown history and supported pending restart indicators. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. A pending restart remains a current review item, while historical degradation directs narrower time-based correlation without becoming a current bottleneck diagnosis or repair authorization.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Hardware operational conclusion\n\nThe Hardware Errors workflow returns one conclusion across WHEA history, Windows Memory Diagnostic history, current Plug and Play device problems and exact physical-disk reliability correlation. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Current or historical concerns direct exact identity and timestamp review without becoming a component diagnosis or repair authorization.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Reliability history conclusion\n\nThe Reliability History workflow returns one conclusion across bounded Reliability Monitor records, recent Application errors and recent unexpected shutdown events. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Completed historical concerns remain informational and direct narrower identity and timestamp review without becoming a current health verdict, root-cause diagnosis or repair authorization.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Driver installation history conclusion\n\nThe Driver Installation History workflow returns one conclusion across retained SetupAPI failed sections and exact current Plug and Play identity matches. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Only a matched present device with a current nonzero configuration code requests review. Historical failures remain informational and never select a driver action.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## System Restore readiness conclusion\n\nThe System Restore workflow returns one conclusion across the bounded restore-point inventory and explicit creation eligibility. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. The conclusion never claims protection coverage or future restore success and does not itself select creation. Creating one point remains a separately confirmed repair with fresh eligibility and exact verification.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Windows Recovery readiness conclusion\n\nThe Windows Recovery workflow correlates enabled REAgentC configuration with the exact currently reported disk and partition, then returns one bounded readiness conclusion. Missing or incomplete location evidence takes precedence. Enabled status and partition presence do not verify image files, BCD correctness, bootability or repair success. Guarded enablement remains limited to an existing disabled configuration.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Peripheral readiness conclusions\n\nBluetooth, camera, smart card, biometric, image acquisition, human interface, portable device, text input and display enhancement workflows return one additional readiness conclusion across current Plug and Play configuration, bounded driver inventory, exact service availability and matching service-failure history. Shell Hardware Detection applies the same conservative model to storage-controller, disk and portable-device evidence. Incomplete evidence takes precedence, and current concerns remain attention without widening repair authority. These conclusions do not prove AutoPlay behavior, media readiness, volume access, physical device operation or end-to-end feature behavior.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Windows Update Medic Service\n\nThe focused `update-medic-service` workflow combines exact current `WaaSMedicSvc` state, registered ServiceDll presence and signature status, bounded current Windows Update activity and exact Service Control Manager failure history into one conservative conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. DLL observations, activity and history never authorize a start or component action. A stopped demand-start state remains informational in every broader workflow. When this exact tool is selected, optional recovery can issue one bounded start only after complete dependency, executable registration, administrator, restart and fresh final evidence checks pass. Starting the service can resume Windows remediation or update activity under the current configuration. KillerBench preserves update policy, source enrollment, schedules, packages, BITS jobs, restart settings, service permissions and startup type. Verification covers stable service state only. It does not claim remediation, component health, applicability, download, installation or restart readiness.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Windows Update Service\n\nThe focused `windows-update-service` workflow combines exact current state, registered ServiceDll presence and signature status, bounded current Windows Update activity and exact Service Control Manager failure history into one conservative conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. DLL observations, activity and history never authorize its separately guarded start. The service can start only from a stopped automatic state after fresh dependency and executable-registration checks. Starting it may resume existing update activity under current policy. Policy, source enrollment, schedules, restart settings, packages, BITS jobs, service permissions and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not establish source reachability, applicability, download, installation, completion or restart readiness. BITS, Cryptographic Services, Windows Modules Installer and Update Orchestrator use their own focused evidence combinations and conservative conclusions.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Delivery Optimization\n\nThe focused `delivery-optimization-service` workflow shows exact service state, privacy-bounded current transfer aggregates, recognized policy surfaces, exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Missing evidence takes precedence, while service, policy or historical concerns remain review-only. Historical failures cannot authorize continuation. Running and stopped demand-start states remain informational. Only an exact Paused current state can offer the separate recovery. Execution repeats that state immediately before one bounded continue request and requires two coherent Running observations. Startup type, cache contents, peers, bandwidth, update source and managed policy remain unchanged. Verification does not claim transfer activity, cache health, peer reachability, update progress or completion.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## BITS service\n\nThe focused `bits-service` workflow shows current-account transfer jobs, exact current service state, bounded exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion before its service recipe is evaluated. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical failures and transfer snapshots cannot authorize a start. Optional recovery still requires exact current stopped-service evidence, dependencies, executable registration, administrator access, clear restart evidence and one unchanged final check. Jobs, priorities, owners, destinations, proxy settings, policy and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not prove transfer progress, application ownership, acknowledgment or completion.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Cryptographic Services\n\nThe focused `cryptographic-service` workflow shows exact current service state, registered service DLL presence and signature status, bounded exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical failures and DLL observations cannot authorize a service start or component replacement. Optional recovery still requires the normal exact-service prerequisites and one unchanged final check. Certificates, keys, catalogs, trust settings, policy and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state and a present Valid DLL do not validate certificates, signatures, catalogs, trust chains, update payloads or application behavior.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Windows Modules Installer\n\nThe focused `modules-installer-service` workflow shows exact current service state, bounded servicing package transitions, exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical failures and package states cannot authorize a service start or package operation. Optional recovery still requires the normal exact-service prerequisites and one unchanged final check. Packages, component-store content, sources, policy and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not establish component-store health, package applicability, servicing completion or restart readiness.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Update Orchestrator\n\nThe focused `update-orchestrator-service` workflow shows exact current service state, the exact built-in Windows Update Scheduled Start task, bounded current Windows Update activity, exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Task results, activity and historical failures cannot authorize a service start or update operation. Optional recovery still requires the normal exact-service prerequisites and one unchanged final check. Policy, source enrollment, schedules, restart settings, packages, BITS jobs, service permissions and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not establish applicability, download progress, installation progress, completion or restart readiness.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Windows Error Reporting\n\nThe focused `error-reporting-service` workflow combines stored crash-dump configuration, bounded dump-file metadata, recent system crash reports, exact Windows Error Reporting service-failure history and current service readiness into one conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical reports and service failures remain context and never authorize recovery or report handling. The workflow does not open dump contents, submit or delete reports, change consent or upload policy, infer root cause or claim report delivery.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Diagnostic Policy\n\nThe focused `diagnostic-policy-service` workflow combines up to 200 Operational events from the last seven days, exact Diagnostic Policy Service failure history and current service readiness into one conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Event summaries group warning, error and critical records by event ID and level while keeping account, process, scenario, troubleshooter and session payload in raw evidence. Historical records cannot authorize service recovery or a troubleshooting action. The workflow does not run a troubleshooter, accept a suggested fix, change policy or troubleshooting data, infer root cause, or claim that diagnosis or repair completed.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Windows Event Collector\n\nThe focused `event-collector-service` workflow combines bounded subscription inventory, exact Windows Event Collector service-failure history and current service readiness into one conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. An empty inventory can be intentional, while subscription counts and historical failures remain context and cannot authorize recovery or subscription changes. The workflow does not establish source reachability, authentication, event delivery, event freshness or subscription health.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Remote Access\n\nThe focused `remote-access-service` workflow combines bounded current-account VPN profile configuration, exact Remote Access Connection Manager service-failure history and current service readiness into one conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. An empty inventory can be intentional, connection labels remain snapshots, and profile presence or historical failures cannot authorize recovery or a connection attempt. The workflow does not expose credentials, initiate a connection or establish profile applicability, server reachability, authentication, tunnel negotiation, route installation, name resolution or connectivity.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Remote Desktop\n\nThe focused `remote-desktop-services` workflow combines local enablement, NLA and listener-port configuration with bounded connection and session history, exact service-failure history and current service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Current NLA or service concerns request review, while retained connection, session and service failures remain historical context and cannot authorize recovery or configuration changes. The workflow does not establish effective policy, listener binding, firewall access, certificate identity, permissions, authentication, network reachability, connection success or session creation.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Windows Remote Management\n\nThe focused `windows-remote-management` workflow combines bounded WinRM listener configuration, operational history, exact service-failure history and current service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Disabled listener registrations or current service concerns request review, while retained events and service failures remain historical context and cannot authorize recovery or configuration changes. The workflow does not establish effective policy, listener binding, firewall access, certificate validity, permissions, authentication, client reachability or remote command execution.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Network Connections\n\nThe focused `network-connections-service` workflow combines bounded current network profile assignments, exact Network Connections service-failure history and current service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Public profiles and historical failures remain context and cannot authorize recovery or a network change. The workflow does not establish adapter availability, address assignment, route selection, name resolution, firewall access, Internet reachability or connection success.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## WinHTTP Auto-Proxy\n\nThe focused `winhttp-autoproxy-service` workflow combines stored WinHTTP defaults, current-account Internet settings, exact service-failure history and current service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Configuration and history cannot authorize recovery or discovery. The workflow does not fetch a URL or establish PAC or WPAD resolution, proxy reachability, authentication, policy ownership, application overrides or Internet access.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Windows Connection Manager\n\nThe focused `connection-manager-service` workflow combines bounded current network profile assignments, exact Windows Connection Manager failure history and current service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Public profiles and historical failures remain context and cannot authorize recovery or a connection decision. The workflow does not establish profile applicability, connection selection, metering, roaming, authentication, routing, name resolution or connectivity.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Network Setup Service\n\nThe focused `network-setup-service` workflow combines bounded current adapter protocol bindings, exact Network Setup Service failure history and current service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. An active hardware adapter with both IPv4 and IPv6 disabled requests review, while binding state and historical failures cannot authorize recovery or a setup change. The workflow does not establish adapter availability, driver suitability, pending setup completion, address assignment, routing, name resolution or connectivity.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Storage Service\n\nThe focused `storage-service` workflow combines bounded current Storage-module jobs, exact Storage Service failure history and current service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Suspended or failed jobs and current service concerns request review, while historical failures remain context. Job state cannot authorize service recovery or storage changes, and a completed job is not an operation-result verdict.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Windows Security Center\n\nThe focused `security-center-service` workflow combines bounded antivirus registrations with raw provider state, exact Security Center service-failure history and current service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. An empty inventory is not a protection-health verdict, and registration count, raw state or historical failures cannot authorize recovery or a security change. The workflow does not establish antivirus presence, protection state, signature freshness, notification delivery, registration ownership or effective policy.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Task Scheduler Service\n\nThe focused `task-scheduler-service` workflow inventories bounded task state, runtime results and missed-run counts, then combines them with exact Task Scheduler service history before separately checking current service availability. Incomplete task inventory or service history remains explicit. Task findings and historical failures request narrower review and cannot authorize a service start. Optional recovery still requires exact current stopped-service evidence, dependencies, executable registration, administrator access, clear restart evidence and one unchanged final check. No task is started, enabled, disabled or changed. Stable service state does not prove task action success, credential validity or future execution.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows += "\n\n## Windows account broker services\n\nThe focused `web-account-manager-service` and `microsoft-account-sign-in-service` workflows combine payload-sanitized AAD Operational history with exact service-failure history and current service readiness. Before evidence is stored, the Engine removes event payload, computer and security identities, correlation data and process data. Only provider, event ID, level, timestamp and record ID remain. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical broker events remain context and cannot authorize a service start or establish current account validity, token availability, authentication, connectivity or application access.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows = window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.workflows.replace(
  "MACE provides a review-only Microsoft application removal plan for explicit OneDrive, Outlook, Office/Microsoft 365, Project and Teams selections. Alongside exact registration evidence, it queries a fixed set of executable names for selected products and retains bounded counts only. Active or incomplete process evidence blocks future removal eligibility. The Engine does not expose process identities or authorize removal from this review.",
  "MACE provides a Microsoft application removal review for explicit OneDrive, Outlook, Office/Microsoft 365, Project and Teams selections. Alongside exact registration evidence, bounded safeguards review selected app processes, OneDrive known-folder containment, product-level credential references, fixed application-data locations and fixed machine or current-account policy areas. They exclude process identities, paths, filenames, contents, policy names or values, usernames, attributes and secrets. Exact provider classification supports preserved data only for an unprotected current-account loose-file development package. Ordinary installed Store/MSIX packages remain blocked because Windows limits this preservation option to development-mode packages. The desktop presents supported candidates in one selector and renders the selected plan in the main evidence display. For the supported boundary, Engine coordination requires preserved data and credentials, clear processes and policy, repeated exact eligibility, durable preparation, a final boundary check, one injected provider request and exact absence verification. Interrupted requests are never repeated automatically. Native execution remains disconnected, and the CLI exposes eligibility preview only."
);
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\n## MACE current-account package coordination\n\n`MacePackageRemoval.Evaluate` checks one exact package identity against a complete `MaceRemovalReview`. `RunAsync` repeats the same provider and safeguard evidence, writes durable preparation, performs a final boundary check, calls one injected `IMacePackageRemovalHost`, and verifies exact absence. The Engine ships no native removal host. `ReconcileAsync` observes an interrupted attempt and never calls removal.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\n## Review MACE package eligibility\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- mace-review --outlook --json\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- mace-package-plan --outlook --package PACKAGE_FULL_NAME --json\n```\n\nThe plan command repeats current MACE inventory and safeguard collection, then evaluates one exact package full name. It is preview-only and connects no removal provider. Preserved-data eligibility is limited to loose-file development packages because Windows does not offer that safeguard for ordinary installed Store/MSIX packages. An eligible result does not remove the package.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\nThe shared workflow-option catalog includes desktop controls for Network, Audio, System Restore, Storage, Printing, Windows Installer, Windows Time, Event Log, Profiles and Computer Name. Printing distinguishes queue repair, default selection and one blocked-job cancellation. Installer accepts only an optional normalized MSI product code. Time validates existing, domain and custom-peer choices. Event Log limits enablement to the four inspected diagnostic channels. Profile controls define the inactivity threshold and optional dependency review without authorizing profile changes. Computer Name requires one valid exact proposed name and keeps rename execution explicit. These definitions preserve each workflow's read-only default and represent execution as a separate advanced control.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\nAdditional validated option examples:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options audio --set direction=Output --set role=Communications\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options storage --set volume=D: --set optimizationReview=true --set optimizationMode=ReTrim\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options printing --set jobQueue=\"Office Printer\" --set jobId=42 --set cancelJob=true\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options installer --set scope=Machine --set productCode={00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001}\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options time --set source=CustomPeers --set \"peers=time.example.test 192.0.2.1\"\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options eventlog --set channel=Setup --set execute=true\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options profiles --set minimumInactiveDays=180 --set checkDependencies=true\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options computer-name --set proposedName=NEW-PC --set execute=true\n```\n\nThese commands validate plans only. They do not inspect or change Windows.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\nBackground Transfers adds a validated running-account or administrator-only all-users inventory scope. This metadata does not authorize observation or retry of a selected job.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\nValidate the all-users BITS inventory scope without collecting it:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options background-transfers --set scope=AllUsers\n```\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\nApp Packages exposes current-account or administrator-only all-users inventory plus optional provisioned-package inventory. Installed Applications exposes machine or running-account registrations. Both remain read-only and do not establish ownership, health or removal eligibility.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\nValidate application inventory scopes without collecting them:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options app-packages --set scope=AllUsers --set includeProvisioned=true\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options installed-apps --set scope=CurrentAccount\n```\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\nSearch and Component Cleanup expose their existing guarded maintenance requests as advanced controls that are off by default. A valid option plan does not bypass fresh evidence, durable preparation, bounded execution, verification or recovery.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\nValidate guarded maintenance requests without executing them:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options search --set rebuildIndex=true\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options component-cleanup --set execute=true\n```\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\nWindows Recovery exposes explicit guarded enablement after exact Disabled evidence. Firewall Services exposes exact Domain, Private and Public profile selections and requires at least one selection before enablement. Both remain diagnosis-only by default and preserve managed settings.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\nValidate recovery and firewall requests without executing them:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options windows-recovery --set execute=true\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options firewall-services --set profile.private=true --set profile.public=true --set execute=true\n```\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\nBitLocker options expose mutually exclusive recovery-file backup, encryption and protection-resume confirmations. Backup and encryption validation requires a matching operation, File destination, absolute path outside the protected volume and one exact nonempty recovery protector GUID. Validation never reads recovery passwords or executes a change.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\nValidate a BitLocker backup request without writing recovery information:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow-options bitlocker --set volume=C: --set operation=BackupRecovery --set backup=File --set backupFile=D:\\recovery.txt --set protectorId=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001 --set executeBackup=true\n```\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\nRead-only shared workflow execution now carries an exact selected Event Log channel and selected Firewall profiles into the main evidence results. Event Log review reports current configuration and policy ownership. Firewall review requires complete Domain, Private and Public ActiveStore evidence before describing selected targets. Neither review exposes a repair identity or authorizes a change.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\nRun selected Event Log and Firewall reviews without changing Windows:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow eventlog --set channel=Setup --json\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow firewall-services --set profile.private=true --set profile.public=true --json\n```\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.api += "\n\nThe shared Computer Name workflow reviews the exact proposed name against active and configured identity, computer role, restart evidence and administrator access. Review-ready evidence remains Attention with no repair identity; the separate guarded coordinator retains rename authority.\n";
window.KillerBenchEngineDocs.cli += "\n\nReview one proposed computer name without requesting a rename:\n\n```powershell\ndotnet run --project Cli\\KillerBench.Cli.csproj -- workflow computer-name --set proposedName=OFFICE-PC --json\n```\n";
